<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagarkurnool district</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nagarkurnool is a district of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, formed in 2016 from part of Mahbubnagar district. It lies along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> river and includes much of the Nallamala forest and the Amrabad Tiger Reserve. In earlier centuries it held small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindari</a> estates, such as Jatprole, under the Qutb Shahi sultans and the Nizams.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nagarkurnool-district.jpg" alt="Nagarkurnool district" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: P. Madhusudan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madhava_Swamy_Temple,_Kollapur_Telangana_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Monuments and inscriptions found near the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and Tungabhadra rivers show that the region has been inhabited since the Stone Age. The early Western Chalukya period lasted from about 535 to about 757 and the Rastrakuta period from 757 to 973; the Eruva Cholas also held it. The Krishna formed the southern boundary of the Kakatiya Empire, and the area later came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-sultanates">Deccan sultanates</a>. Local tradition holds that Gona Ganna Reddy, an agent of Rani Rudrama Devi, ruled from Nandi Vardhamana Puram, near Nagarkurnool.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The district covers 6,545 square kilometres of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a> and the northern Nallamala Hills, a landscape of granite rocks and hills at an average elevation of 458 metres. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> thorn scrub and dry deciduous forests cover much of it, and the semi-arid climate leaves farming dependent on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> runs along the southern border, and chains of linked lakes and ponds drain into the Dindubi river. The southern forests belong to the Nagarjunsagar-Srisailam Tiger Reserve, where the state has developed a jungle safari at Farahbad and river cottages near Somasila and Eegalapenta.</p><p>Agriculture is the mainstay of the economy, with paddy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulses">pulses</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and groundnut in places, and mango and citrus orchards, supported by the Kalwakurthy Lift Irrigation Scheme. Rice mills, seed processing and handloom weaving are the main small industries. The district has no railway, but national highways link Kalwakurthy with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> and Srisailam.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town of Nagarkurnool is the headquarters of a district of four revenue divisions, Achampet, Nagarkurnool, Kalwakurthy and Kollapur, and 20 mandals. The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 861,766 people, about a fifth of them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-castes">Scheduled Castes</a> and an eighth from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-tribes">Scheduled Tribes</a>. Telugu is the mother tongue of most, followed by Lambadi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>, and the great majority are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nagarkurnool district” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By about 1600 they held lands in the Krishna valley, in what is now <b>Nagarkurnool district</b>, and kept them under the Qutb Shahis and the Nizams.</blockquote><a href="/state/jatprole-samasthan">Jatprole Samasthan</a></li></ul></section>
